# Arc Foundry — The assets you produce

Arc Foundry ships with no art, no effects, and no sound. You produce every asset the game
plays, commit the produced files, and wire them into the build. This file is the
contract: what to produce, which tool makes it, where the file lands, and the bar it
meets.

Six asset-generation binaries are on your `PATH` in this container. They are there for
this build alone: the finished repository installs and builds with none of them present
and with no network access, so the build bundles the committed files and generates
nothing at build time.

## The six tools

These six binaries are on your `PATH`, and no others. There is no tool for user
interface, painting, textures, voxels, or meshes, so every panel, menu, and overlay is
drawn in code.

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | One sprite, as a PNG | The yard, the components, the combination towers, the blocker, the projectiles, the Load, and the icons |
| `draw-sheet` | A sprite sequence, one PNG per frame | The Load's idle cycles, the firing cycles, and the press cycle |
| `particle-2d` | A particle system, as a `system.json` | Every electrical effect |
| `sfx-synth` | A synthesized sound, as a `.wav` | The sharper cues |
| `sfx-sample` | A sampled sound over a baked pack, as a `.wav` | The fuller cues |
| `music` | Sequenced music over a baked bank, as a `.wav` and a `.mid` | The music bed |

Each is a command-line tool. Run `<tool> --help` for its operations, and
`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags. In outline each records the
operations you issue and then renders the finished file to a path you name.

- `draw` and `draw-sheet` rasterize a fixed-size straight-alpha RGBA canvas from drawing
  operations. `draw-sheet` is `draw` with a frame index on every operation, and it emits
  one separate PNG per frame rather than a strip.
- `particle-2d` authors a system as emitters, forces, and per-particle curves, and emits
  the `system.json` that is the asset. It bakes no frames.
- `sfx-synth`, `sfx-sample`, and `music` render a PCM `.wav`. `sfx-sample` and `music`
  draw on a baked pack already in the container, which their help describes.

## Where the files land

Every produced file lands under `assets/` at the repository root, at the exact path
below. Sprite frames are named by their frame index, `0.png` upward.

```
assets/
  yard/substrate.png  entry.png  collector.png  waypoint.png  housing.png
  components/<type>/base.png
  components/<type>/head-<tier>.png
  components/<type>/fire/0.png .. 3.png
  combos/<id>/base.png  head.png  fire/0.png .. 3.png
  blocker.png
  projectiles/<type>.png
  load/<type>/0.png .. 3.png
  press/0.png .. 3.png
  icons/charge.png  integrity.png  type-<type>.png
  fx/<effect>.json
  audio/<cue>.wav
  audio/music.mid
```

`<type>` is a base component's identifier or a Load type's identifier, `<id>` is a
combination tower's identifier, `<tier>` is `1` through `5`, `<effect>` is one of the
twelve named below, and `<cue>` is one of the twelve cue names in `specs/ui.md`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### Loading them

The build loads its own files. The built site is served from any base path, at the root
of a static host or mounted under a sub-path of one, so every URL the build requests
resolves against the page rather than against the origin root: no request carries a
leading `/`, and the bundler's base is relative. The built site is self-contained and
carries every file it draws and plays.
{{else}}
### Loading them

The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader, documented in the `engine/`
directory at the root of this repository. It resolves every path under one root,
`assets/`, relative to the page the build is served from, and it fetches and decodes the
file. Load each file through that loader rather than requesting a URL of your own,
writing the path relative to that root: `components/capacitor/head-3.png`,
`fx/chain.json`, `load/mote/0.png`.

The built site serves every produced file under that root, so a path the loader resolves
reaches the file at whatever base path the site is served from.
{{/if}}

## Sprites

Produce each with `draw`, on a small transparent straight-alpha canvas. These are pixel
art: draw at the sprite's native size and sample it with nearest-neighbor so it stays
crisp at every scale the stage is fitted to.

| Sprite | Path | Size | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Yard substrate | `yard/substrate.png` | `40 x 40` | The tile the yard is tiled from. It repeats seamlessly. |
| Entry | `yard/entry.png` | `40 x 40` | The blown feeder vent the Load spills from. |
| Collector | `yard/collector.png` | `40 x 40` | The grounding sink units ground out at. |
| Waypoint | `yard/waypoint.png` | `20 x 20` | The checkpoint stud drawn on a waypoint platform's tiles. |
| Housing | `yard/housing.png` | `40 x 40` | The transformer box that fills a Fixed-blocked tile. It repeats seamlessly. |
| Component base | `components/<type>/base.png` | `40 x 40` | The fixed mount a head turns on, one per base type. |
| Component head | `components/<type>/head-<tier>.png` | `40 x 40` | The rotating head, one per base type per tier: eight types by five tiers, forty sprites. |
| Combination base | `combos/<id>/base.png` | `40 x 40` | The fixed mount, one per tower. |
| Combination head | `combos/<id>/head.png` | `40 x 40` | The rotating head, one per tower. A tower has no tier variants. |
| Blocker | `blocker.png` | `40 x 40` | The inert fused rock an unharvested candidate hardens into. |
| Projectile | `projectiles/<type>.png` | `12 x 12` | The traveling shot, one per firing base type: seven sprites. |
| Load unit | `load/<type>/0.png` | `20 x 20`, the Slug and the Dynamo `32 x 32` | The unit's first idle frame, which doubles as its still sprite. |
| Charge icon | `icons/charge.png` | `16 x 16` | The status bar's Charge mark. |
| Integrity icon | `icons/integrity.png` | `16 x 16` | The status bar's Grid Integrity mark. |
| Type icon | `icons/type-<type>.png` | `16 x 16` | One glyph per base component type, for the panel. |

Every head, and every projectile, is drawn facing one canonical direction, toward `+x`,
so the game rotates it to its heading. A head's canonical facing is the same across its
five tiers. The Regulator never fires, so its head is a static aura emitter with no
muzzle, drawn across its five tiers like every other type.

The bar these sprites meet:

- A component reads as its type and as its tier. The five tiers escalate in finish, from
  pitted and rusted at Scrap to mirror-chromed at Tesla-Prime.
- A combination tower reads as a combination tower beside any base component, and its
  look reads its dominant ability.
- A blocker reads as dead: no head, no muzzle, no glow.
- Each of the six Load types reads as its own type, the Filament reads as airborne, and
  the Dynamo reads as an unstable overload core.

## Animation frames

Produce each cycle with `draw-sheet`, which emits one PNG per frame. Each cycle below is
four frames, `0.png` through `3.png`, played as a loop.

| Cycle | Path | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Load idle | `load/<type>/0.png` .. `3.png` | The unit seething with charge as it travels. One cycle per Load type, six in all. The Dynamo's carries a visible overload wobble. |
| Component firing | `components/<type>/fire/0.png` .. `3.png` | The head's charge-and-discharge, played once per shot. One cycle per firing base type, seven in all. |
| Regulator idle | `components/regulator/fire/0.png` .. `3.png` | The Regulator's slow aura pulse, played as a loop rather than on a shot, since it never fires. |
| Combination firing | `combos/<id>/fire/0.png` .. `3.png` | The tower's discharge, played once per shot. One cycle per tower, twelve in all. |
| Press | `press/0.png` .. `3.png` | The press stamping, played when a rock is placed. |

A firing cycle is played on the frame a structure fires and runs once. An idle cycle
loops while its subject is on the yard. A body may be reused across tints; what the
cycles must deliver is that the Load visibly crackles, a firing structure visibly charges
and discharges, and the Dynamo visibly seethes.

## Particle systems

Author each with `particle-2d`, whose emit step writes the `system.json` that is the
asset. Each is played live and simulated as it plays, so it varies from one firing to the
next.

Produce these twelve, each at `assets/fx/<effect>.json`:

| Effect | File | Spawned when | What it carries |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Build spark | `fx/build.json` | A rock lands | A shower of sparks and a snap of arc at the new footprint |
| Combine flash | `fx/combine.json` | A combine of either kind resolves | A convergent flash at the resulting structure's footprint |
| Arc bolt | `fx/bolt.json` | A Capacitor, Choke, Rectifier, or Discharge Rig projectile travels | A crackling bolt trail from the head to the target |
| Chain lightning | `fx/chain.json` | A Coil's hit chains | Forked lightning between each unit the chain strikes, dimming per leap |
| Spark spray | `fx/spray.json` | An Emitter fires | A fast fan of small sparks toward the target |
| Discharge ring | `fx/ring.json` | An Arc-Node's shot lands | An expanding ring covering the splash radius |
| Impact | `fx/impact.json` | Any shot connects with a unit | A small burst of sparks at the point of impact |
| Death burst | `fx/death.json` | A unit dies | An electrical pop, scaled up for a Dynamo |
| Leak alarm | `fx/leak.json` | A unit grounds out at the collector | A warning surge at the collector |
| Slow snap | `fx/slow.json` | A slow is applied | A drag snap clinging to the slowed unit |
| Burn | `fx/burn.json` | A burn is applied | An ember flare on impact and a low flicker while the burn ticks |
| Aura pulse | `fx/aura.json` | A structure carrying an aura stands on the yard | A slow pulse ring at the source, marking the aura it projects |

The slow, burn, and aura systems each carry a color of their own, distinct from each
other and from the firing effects, so a player reads which effect is on a unit without
reading a number.

A crit reuses the impact system at a larger scale, and a multishot is several ordinary
projectiles, each carrying its own impact. Neither needs a system of its own.

The firing effects escalate with the firing structure's quality tier, so the ladder reads
in the effects as well as in the sprites: a Scrap bolt is thin and dim where a
Tesla-Prime bolt is fat and bright, and the same escalation applies to the chain, the
spray, the ring, and the arc bolt.

### Playing them

The particle runtime is already a dependency of the project. Import it by its package
name; there is nothing to add to `package.json` for it and nothing to install, and it is
neither fetched nor reimplemented.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
Use its canvas binding: construct a player from a parsed `system.json` and a 2D context,
and advance it each frame with the frame's delta. The engine's declarative pipeline does
not draw particles, so the effects are drawn from a draw component whose `draw` is handed
the raw context. The package's own types are the authoritative API.
{{else}}
Use its canvas binding: construct a player from a parsed `system.json` and your 2D
context, and advance it each frame with the frame's delta. It simulates the system and
composites the particles. The package's own types are the authoritative API.
{{/if}}

Spawn an instance of the matching system at the position of the event that raised it: the
build spark at the stamped footprint, the combine flash at the resulting structure, the
bolt along the line from a firing head to its target, the chain threaded through each
unit a Coil's hit strikes, the ring centered on an Arc-Node's impact point, the impact
where a shot connects, the death burst where a unit dies, the leak alarm at the
collector, the slow snap and the burn on the unit carrying them, and the aura pulse at
its source.

## Audio

Produce twelve `.wav` files, one per cue in `specs/ui.md`, at `assets/audio/<cue>.wav`.
`music` also emits a `.mid` score beside its `.wav`; commit it at `assets/audio/music.mid`
and play the `.wav`.

| Cue | Tool | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `stamp` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | A rock dropping from the press. |
| `fire-bolt` | `sfx-synth` | A sharp single zap. |
| `fire-spark` | `sfx-synth` | A short, light spark, played often. |
| `fire-chain` | `sfx-sample` | A crackling chain. |
| `fire-discharge` | `sfx-sample` | A heavy discharge. |
| `combine` | `sfx-sample` | The chime of a fold resolving. It doubles as a combination tower's spawn cue. |
| `kill` | `sfx-synth` | A unit destroyed. |
| `leak` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | The alarm as Grid Integrity drops. |
| `slow` | `sfx-synth` | A cold shimmer as a slow lands. |
| `burn` | `sfx-synth` | A sizzle as a burn lands. |
| `settle` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` | Unharvested rocks hardening into blockers. |
| `music` | `music` | A tense, driving industrial bed, looped under the yard. |

The eleven effect cues are told apart by ear. `fire-spark` plays several times a second
at a high fire rate, so it is short and does not build up.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Load each `.wav` the same page-relative way as every other produced file, decode it with
the Web Audio API, and play it on the event `specs/ui.md` names. Audio does not start
before the player's first interaction with the page, and the mute control silences the
whole bus.
{{else}}
Bind each `.wav` to its cue name through the engine's audio bus, which loads and decodes
it through the same asset loader, and play the cue by name on the event `specs/ui.md`
names. The engine owns the first-interaction unlock and the mute bit.
{{/if}}

## What is drawn in code

No tool produces the following, and the build draws each of them:

| Drawn in code | Fixed by |
| --- | --- |
| The status bar and the build panel, their text, and their layout | `specs/hud.md` |
| The recipe book and the damage leaderboard | `specs/hud.md` |
| Every screen and menu | `specs/ui.md` |
| The faint tile grid over the yard substrate | `specs/yard.md` |
| The held rock's snapped footprint and its legal or illegal read | `specs/controls.md` |
| The range ring on a held or selected structure | `specs/components.md` |
| Each unit's health bar | `specs/enemies.md` |
| The waypoint order numbers and the flow toward the collector | `specs/pathing.md` |
| The mark on a combinable structure and on the selected fold | `specs/hud.md` |
| The candidate treatment that distinguishes an unharvested roll from a component | `specs/scrap-press.md` |
| Placing the produced yard sprites for each map's topology | `specs/yard.md` |

Everything the game shows or plays traces either to a file produced with one of the six
tools or to one of the code-drawn elements above.
